      "dom_id": "question-34502",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{x+2}-4.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{x+2}-4,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{x+2}+4}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{x+2}-4}{\\sqrt{x+2}-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2}+4)(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{(\\sqrt{x+2})^{2}-{4}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x+2-16} & = \\\\ \\frac{x(\\sqrt{x+2}-4)}{x-14}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
