      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{y+3}+2.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{y+3}+2,$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{y+3}-2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{y+3}+2}{\\sqrt{y+3}+2} & = \\\\ \\frac{5(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)}{(\\sqrt{y+3}-2)(\\sqrt{y+3}+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{(\\sqrt{y+3})^{2}-{2}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y+3-4} & = \\\\ \\frac{5\\sqrt{y+3}+10}{y-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
