      "name": null,
      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}}{\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(\\sqrt{r+2}-\\sqrt{r+4})(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(\\sqrt{r+2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{r+4})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(r+2)-(r+4)} & = \\\\ \\frac{10(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})}{(-2)} & = \\\\ -5(\\sqrt{r+2}+\\sqrt{r+4})\\end{aligned}\n$$",
