      "problem": "Rationalize the denominator of $\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the denominator's conjugate is $\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}.$\n\nNow, to rationalize the denominator, we multiply both the numerator and denominator by the conjugate $\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z},$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z}{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}}{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{(\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{10z})(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}-(\\sqrt{10z})^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{2-10z} & = \\\\ \\frac{5z(\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{10z})}{2(1-5z)}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
